<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael II Asen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1239–<a href="/years/1256">1256</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael II Asen was tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1246 to 1256, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> and Irene Komnene Doukaina. He came to the throne as a child, and during his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> took much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia. His attempts to recover them failed, and in 1256 he was murdered by his cousin Kaliman, ending the direct Asen line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-ii-asen.jpg" alt="Michael II Asen" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael-Asen-Kastoria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Irene Komnene Doukaina, and was most probably born in 1239. His father died in the first half of 1241 and was succeeded by Michael's seven-year-old half-brother Kaliman, who died unexpectedly, possibly of poisoning according to contemporaneous rumours, in August or September 1246.</p><p>Michael was only seven or eight when he succeeded. According to one scholarly theory his mother ruled during his minority, but she stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> just three months after his coronation. According to another, his brother-in-law, the Sevastokrator Peter, assumed the regency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Soon after Kaliman's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a>, Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melnik">Melnik</a>, the latter with local support, and occupied the fortresses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Vardar</a> valley, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II Komnenos Doukas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> occupied western Macedonia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> acknowledged the conquests in a treaty of late 1246 or early 1247 that also bound them to support Vatatzes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and Bulgarian troops helped him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in August 1247. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a> listed Michael's realms among the countries paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><p>In 1254 Michael allied with the Republic of Ragusa against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-i">Stefan Uroš I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and, after Radoslav of Hum joined, campaigned as far as Bijelo Polje. When Vatatzes died on 4 November 1254, Michael broke into Macedonia and reconquered the lands lost to him. The historian George Akropolites recorded that the Bulgarian-speaking inhabitants supported him because they wanted to shake off the 'yoke of those who spoke another language'. To secure peace with Hungary Michael married Anna of Macsó, a granddaughter of Béla IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vatatzes's son and successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris">Theodore II Laskaris</a>, counter-invaded in early 1255 and captured Stara Zagora, then occupied most fortresses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodope-mountains">Rhodope Mountains</a> in the spring. In spring 1256 Michael pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, but the Nicene army defeated his Cuman troops. In June he asked his father-in-law to mediate, and Theodore agreed to peace only after Michael accepted the loss of the lands he had claimed, with the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> as the border.</p><p>The treaty outraged many boyars, who decided to replace Michael with his cousin Kaliman Asen. Kaliman and his allies attacked the tsar, who died of his wounds. Akropolites says he was mortally wounded by his first cousin Kaliman outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael II Asen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1250" aria-label="Veliko Tarnovo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Michael II Asen"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4727 3886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4727 3886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1250">What was here in 1250? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
